<h2 id="why">Why the displayed specs are not enough</h2>
<p>
  In Android's settings, RAM and storage are values that the system <em>declares</em>. On a number
  of cheap boxes these values are rewritten in the firmware: a box with 1 GB of RAM can display 4
  GB. Diagnostic apps that run under Android mostly read what the kernel tells them, so they can be
  fooled in the same way. The free <em>TV Box Specs</em> app (TV Box Stop) remains useful as a
  complementary tool, as long as you remember that the RAM it shows comes from the kernel.
</p>
<p class="notice">
  A discrepancy is not always a fraud: a mistake in the hardware configuration file (DTB) can make a
  box show half the RAM it actually has. Check before drawing conclusions.
</p>

<h2 id="compare">Step 1: compare with the SoC's limits</h2>
<p>
  Every SoC has a maximum amount of RAM it can address. If the advertised figure exceeds that limit,
  it is impossible without having to open anything. This is what the
  <a href="/en/">checker</a> on this site does, relying on the <a href="/en/soc/">SoC pages</a>.
  Look closely at the status of each value: "verified" means a manufacturer datasheet was read or
  several independent sources agree, "community reports" means the limit remains to be confirmed.
</p>

<h2 id="chips">Step 2: open the case and read the chips</h2>
<p>
  This is the most direct method. Unplug the box, open the case (often a few screws or clips under
  the rubber feet) and locate:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>The SoC</strong>: the largest chip, often under a small heatsink. Its marking (for
    example Amlogic, Rockchip or Allwinner followed by a reference) must match the advertised one.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The RAM chips</strong>: usually 1 to 8 black rectangular chips around the SoC, marked
    Samsung, Micron, SK hynix, Nanya, etc.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The storage chip (eMMC)</strong>: a square chip with a capacity reference.
  </li>
</ul>
<h3>Working out the RAM capacity</h3>
<p>
  Note the reference printed on each RAM chip, look up its datasheet (the reference alone is enough
  in a search engine) to find the density of one chip, then multiply by the number of chips.
  Example: four 4-gigabit (Gb) chips make 16 Gb, that is 2 gigabytes (GB). Do not confuse gigabits
  (Gb) with gigabytes (GB): divide by 8.
</p>
<h3>Photographing the chips properly</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Raking light, no glare, a sharp photo of each chip taken head-on.</li>
  <li>Several shots if the marking is worn or very small.</li>
  <li>One overall photo of the board, to count the chips.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="linux">Step 3: boot a Linux system</h2>
<p>
  Booting Linux (for example Armbian or CoreELEC) from an SD card or a USB stick, without touching
  the internal memory, lets you read the RAM as the hardware sees it. Under Linux, two commands are
  enough:
</p>
<pre><code>free -m
cat /proc/meminfo | head -3</code></pre>
<p>
  For the internal storage and the SoC, <code>lsblk</code> and <code>cat /proc/cpuinfo</code> give
  additional hints. An important caveat: the visible RAM also depends on the hardware configuration
  file (DTB) you chose. If a box recognised with 1 GB seems to have 2 GB according to its chips, try
  another DTB before concluding that the advertisement is false.
</p>
<p class="notice">
  Avoid installing an unsupported system on the internal memory until you have backed up the
  original firmware: a failed installation can make the box unusable.
</p>

<h2 id="uart">Step 4: read the boot log (UART)</h2>
<p>
  Many boards expose three or four contact points (TX, RX, GND) for a serial link. With a 3.3 V
  USB-serial adapter you can read the bootloader log from power-on. This log usually shows the
  amount of RAM detected before Android even starts, which makes it strong evidence. Connect only
  TX, RX and GND, never the 5 V, and check the signal voltage (3.3 V) before connecting the adapter.
</p>

<h2 id="storage">Step 5: test the storage</h2>
<p>
  A faked storage advertises a capacity greater than the real one: writes beyond the real capacity
  wrap around to the start of the memory and overwrite older data. Some tools write test data over
  the whole capacity and then read it back to detect this:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>f3</strong> (<code>f3write</code> and <code>f3read</code>) on Linux or macOS;
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>H2testw</strong> on Windows, for a card or a disk mounted on the PC.
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
  A full test is slow and erases the data on the tested medium: run it on an emptied one. For an
  internal eMMC, boot from an SD card and test the data partition.
</p>

<h2 id="interpret">Reading the results without being fooled</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Strong evidence</strong>: a legible photo of the chips, a UART log, a full storage test.
    These allow a case to be qualified as "confirmed" on this site.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Medium evidence</strong>: reading under Linux or Armbian. Reliable if the DTB file
    really matches the board.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Weak evidence</strong>: anything read from Android alone.
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
  Sellers sometimes change components (RAM, Wi-Fi, board revision) without changing the model name:
  two boxes with the same name are not necessarily identical.
</p>

